<!--
  العربية.

  Written to match the English in register, not just in meaning. This app's English is deliberately
  personal — it speaks to one person, plainly, and never like a form. The Arabic is فصحى so anyone
  can read it, but second-person and unadorned for the same reason.

  Three rules:

  1. Every key in values/strings.xml must exist here. StringResourcesTest fails the build otherwise
     — a missing key does not error at runtime, it silently drops one English sentence into the
     middle of an Arabic screen.
  2. Every <plurals> carries all six Arabic forms: zero one two few many other. Arabic changes the
     noun at 1, 2, 3-10, 11-99 and 100+, which is the whole reason counts are not concatenated.
  3. Placeholders are copied exactly (%1$s, %2$s) and never reordered by hand — the numbering is
     what keeps them attached to the right value when the sentence order changes.

  Numbers stay Western (1, 2, 3) rather than ٠١٢٣: the counter draws its big figures from Kotlin,
  which is always Western, and two systems on one screen reads as a bug.
-->
